['The problem at hand requires advanced knowledge of Java IO, multithreading, design patterns (specifically for caching), and integration with an external logging service. Here is a basic code skeleton to implement the solution. Please note that actual implementation may vary based on your use case such as specific MoSTState class definition, external logging service usage etc.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class StateLoader {
    private static final int CACHE_SIZE = 100; // adjust this according to your needs
    private ConcurrentHashMap<String, MoSTState> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public MoSTState load(String filePath) {
        if (cache.containsKey(filePath)) {
            return cache.get(filePath);
        }

        FileInputStream fis;
        ObjectInputStream ois = null;
        try {
            fis = new FileInputStream(filePath);
            ois = new ObjectInputStream(fis);
            MoSTState state = (MoSTState) ois.readObject();

            if (cache.size() >= CACHE_SIZE) {
                cache.clear(); // remove all instances of MoSTState in memory to prevent excessive memory usage
            }
            cache.put(filePath, state);

            return state;
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        } finally {
            if (ois != null) {
                try {
                    ois.close();
                } catch (IOException ex) {
                    ex.printStackTrace();
                }
            }
        }

        return null;
    }

    // Here's how you might log events using a hypothetical logging service
    private void log(String message) {
        ExternalLoggingService.logMessage("StateLoader: " + message);
    }
}
```

In this code, the `ConcurrentHashMap` is used as an in-memory cache to limit the number of instances of MoSTState that can be loaded simultaneously. If the map size exceeds a certain threshold (determined by CACHE_SIZE), all cached objects are cleared out to prevent excessive memory usage. The method is thread-safe due to the use of `ConcurrentHashMap`, which provides synchronization for multiple threads attempting to access and modify it concurrently.

