['In order to accomplish these requirements in Rust, you can use the `reqwest` crate as it provides a comprehensive set of features such as HTTP/1 and HTTP/2 support, HTTPS with optional certificate verification, connection pooling, request and response metrics logging, and error handling. Here is an example implementation:

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use reqwest::Url;

async fn send_request(url: &str, method: &str, headers: HashMap<&str, &str>, body: Option<String>) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    // Create a HTTP client builder
    let mut client = reqwest::ClientBuilder::new()
        .user_agent("my-http-client")
        // Disable certificate verification for HTTPS connections (not recommended)
        .danger_accept_invalid_certs(true)
        // Enable connection pooling to handle multiple requests concurrently efficiently
        .pool_max_idle_per_host(50)
        .build()?;

    // Create a request builder for the given URL and HTTP method
    let mut request = match *method {
        "GET" => client.get(&url),
        "POST" => client.post(&url),
        _ => return Err("Invalid HTTP method".into()),
    };

    // Add headers to the request
    for (key, value) in &headers {
        request = request.header(key, value);
    }

    // For POST requests, add a body
    if let Some(body) = body {
        request = request.body(body);
    }

    // Send the request and handle the response or error cases
    match request.send().await {
        Ok(response) => println_request_metrics(&url, &method, headers, &body)?,
        Err(err) => return Err(format_error("Request failed", err)),
    }

    Ok(())
}
```

Note: You'll also need to add the `reqwest` dependency to your Cargo.toml file:
```toml
[dependencies]
reqwest = { version = "0.11", features = ["json"] }
tokio = { version = "1", features = ["full"] }
``` 

Remember to handle errors and edge cases according to your specific requirements. Also, the code assumes that all URLs are valid and secure (HTTPS), which may not be the case in a production environment. Always validate URLs and consider using a more robust solution for handling HTTP requests in Rust.
