public class IntegerRandomValueGenerator : RandomValueGenerator<int>
{
    public IntegerRandomValueGenerator(int seed, int min = int.MinValue, int max = int.MaxValue) : base(seed, min, max)
    { }

    protected override int NextRandom()
    {
        return _random.Next(Min, Max);
    }
}

public class FloatRandomValueGenerator : RandomValueGenerator<float>
{
    public FloatRandomValueGenerator(int seed, float min = float.MinValue, float max = float.MaxValue) : base(seed, min, max)
    { }

    protected override float NextRandom()
    {
        return (float)(_random.NextDouble() * (_random.Next() - Min) + Min);
    }
}
```
This code creates a base abstract class `RandomValueGenerator` that other concrete classes will inherit from. Each of the concrete classes must implement an abstract method called `NextRandom`, which generates the next random value in their respective types (`int` or `float`). They also call upon the base constructor with necessary arguments.

For reproducibility across runs, each generator has its own instance of `System.Random` initialized with a specific seed. When resetting all generators to initial states, new seeds are generated and assigned to generators' `System.Random` instances.

Each concrete generator also overrides the base class's `ToString` method that provides detailed string representation including each generator's current state, i.e., its seed, minimum and maximum values, and the last generated value.
']

['Here's an example solution using JavaScript ES6 and Node.js. This code uses the `async` package for flow control and Promises for async operations. For simplicity, I am just mocking the network requests with setTimeout and a random value generator. Replace this with your actual data fetching mechanism.

```javascript
const EventEmitter = require('events');
const AsyncLock = require('async-lock');

class DataAggregator extends EventEmitter {
  constructor(initialValue, aggregationFunction) {
    super();
    this.initialValue = initialValue;
    this.aggregateValue = initialValue;
    this.aggregationFunction = aggregationFunction;
    this.lock = new AsyncLock();
  }

  async _getDataChunk() {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => setTimeout(() => Math.random() < 0.9 ? resolve(Math.floor(Math.random() * 10)) : reject('Network error'), 500));
  }

  async processNextChunk() {
    try {
      const data = await this._getDataChunk();

      if (this.lock.isBusy()) {
        return; // Avoid processing if paused
      }

      await this.lock.acquire('aggregate', async () => {
        this.aggregateValue = this.aggregationFunction(this.aggregateValue, data);
        this.emit('progress', this.aggregateValue);
      });

    } catch (error) {
      console.log("Retrying due to error:", error);
      return this.processNextChunk(); // Retry on errors
    } 

    this.processNextChunk(); // Recursively process next chunk
  }

  start() {
    if (!this.lock.isLocked('aggregate')) {
      this.processNextChunk();
    } else {
      console.log("Already started, ignoring");
    }
  }

  pause() {
    this.lock.disable();
  }

  resume() {
    if (this.lock.isDisabled()) {
      this.lock.enable();
      this.processNextChunk(); // Resume processing if was paused before
    } else {
      console.log("Already started, ignoring");
    }
  }
}
```

You can use it like this:

```javascript
const aggregator = new DataAggregator(0, (a, b) => a + b); // Summing up numbers for example

aggregator.on('progress', progress => {
  console.log("Progress: ", progress);
});

aggregator.start();
// ...
aggregator.pause();
// ...
aggregator.resume();
```

Note that this is a simplified example and might not fully meet your requirements. In a real-world scenario, you would need to handle schema modifications or concurrent updates in the `aggregationFunction` itself (or another dedicated function), as well as adjust the timeout value in `_getDataChunk` for network delays.
']

