['Here is a simple function that performs MD5 hashing in chunks of 4096 bytes (standard buffer size). This approach provides good balance between speed, memory usage and the ability to handle very large inputs. The function uses `Stream` and `CryptoStream` from `System.Security.Cryptography` namespace for reading the input data in chunks.

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Text;
using System.Security.Cryptography;

public class HashingService
{
    private const int BufferSize = 4096;

    public string GetMD5Hash(string input)
    {
        using (var md5 = MD5.Create())
        {
            byte[] hashBytes;

            // If the input is a file, read it in chunks
            if (File.Exists(input))
            {
                using (var stream = File.OpenRead(input))
                    hashBytes = ComputeHashFromStream(md5, stream);
            }
            else
            {
                // Treat the input as a string and read it in chunks
                var bytes = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(input);
                using (var stream = new MemoryStream(bytes))
                    hashBytes = ComputeHashFromStream(md5, stream);
            }

            // Convert the byte array to hexadecimal string
            var sb = new StringBuilder();
            for (int i = 0; i < hashBytes.Length; i++)
                sb.Append(hashBytes[i].ToString("X2"));

            return sb.ToString();
        }
    }

    private byte[] ComputeHashFromStream(MD5 md5, Stream stream)
    {
        var buffer = new byte[BufferSize];

        // Read the file in chunks and compute MD5 hash for each chunk
        int read;
        while ((read = stream.Read(buffer, 0, BufferSize)) > 0)
            md5.TransformBlock(buffer, 0, read, buffer, 0);

        // Finalize the computation of the hash
        md5.TransformFinalBlock(buffer, 0, 0);

        return md5.Hash;
    }
}
```

This function uses `MD5` from `System.Security.Cryptography` namespace to perform MD5 hashing in a streaming manner. The `ComputeHashFromStream` method reads the input data in chunks and updates the hash for each chunk using `TransformBlock` method. After reading all the chunks, it finalizes the computation of the hash using `TransformFinalBlock` method.

You can use this function as follows:
```csharp
var hashingService = new HashingService();
string md5Hash = hashingService.GetMD5Hash("Hello World");
Console.WriteLine(md5Hash);
```

Note that while this function works efficiently with very large inputs, it might not be suitable for all use cases and performance can depend on the specifics of the environment (CPU speed, disk I/O, etc.). Also, there is no logging integration in this code as it's quite complex to integrate such functionality into the existing system without knowing the exact requirements.
']

['Here is your requested C# code:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Text;

public class DecryptionException : Exception
{
    public DecryptionException(string message) : base(message) { }
}

public static class AESDecrypter
{
    private const int BlockSize = 16; // 128 bits

    public static byte[] DecryptAESCBC(byte[] key, byte[] iv, byte[] ciphertext)
    {
        if (!IsValidKeyLength(key))
            throw new ArgumentException("Invalid key length. Expected lengths: 16, 24 or 32 bytes.");

        if (iv.Length != BlockSize)
            throw new ArgumentException($"Invalid IV length. Expected length: {BlockSize} bytes.");

        byte[] decryptedData;

        using (var aes = Aes.Create())
        {
            aes.Key = key;
            aes.IV = iv;
            aes.Padding = PaddingMode.PKCS7;
            aes.Mode = CipherMode.CBC;

            var decryptor = aes.CreateDecryptor(aes.Key, aes.IV);

            using (var msDecrypt = new MemoryStream())
            {
                using (var csDecrypt = new CryptoStream(msDecrypt, decryptor, CryptoStreamMode.Write))
                {
                    try
                    {
                        csDecrypt.Write(ciphertext, 0, ciphertext.Length);
                        csDecrypt.FlushFinalBlock(); // This will throw an exception if the padding is incorrect

                        decryptedData = msDecrypt.ToArray();
                    }
                    catch (CryptographicException e)
                    {
                        throw new DecryptionException("Invalid padding or decryption failed: " + e.Message);
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return RemovePadding(decryptedData);
    }

    private static bool IsValidKeyLength(byte[] key) => key.Length == 16 || key.Length == 24 || key.Length == 32;

    private static byte[] RemovePadding(byte[] decryptedBytes)
    {
        int paddingByte = decryptedBytes[^1]; // Last byte represents the number of bytes used for padding

        if (paddingByte < 1 || paddingByte > BlockSize)
            throw new DecryptionException("Invalid padding");

        // Check that all bytes in the last block are the same, which means it's padding
        int start = decryptedBytes.Length - paddingByte;
        for (int i = 0; i < paddingByte; ++i)
        {
            if (decryptedBytes[start + i] != paddingByte)
                throw new DecryptionException("Invalid padding");
        }

        // Create a new array without the padding bytes
        var result = new byte[decryptedBytes.Length - paddingByte];
        Buffer.BlockCopy(decryptedBytes, 0, result, 0, result.Length);

        return result;
    }
}
```
This code creates a function `DecryptAESCBC` that decrypts data using AES-CBC with the provided key and IV. It also checks if padding is valid before returning the decrypted bytes. The `RemovePadding` method verifies correctness of the padding by checking if all padding bytes are equal to their expected value, then creates a new array without the padding bytes. 

This code does not handle hex strings or base64-encoded strings for the key, IV, and ciphertext; you would need to add additional methods for these conversions. Also, to ensure thread safety, consider using `ConcurrentDictionary` or other thread-safe collections where appropriate in a multi-threaded environment.
']

