['Due to the complexity of this task, a complete implementation would be quite large. However, I will provide some basic structure on how it could look like in C:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#define MAX_QUEUE_SIZE 1024 // adjust according to your needs

typedef struct {
    int timestamp;
    int eventType;
    char *data;
    int priority;
} Event;

typedef void (*EventHandler)(Event*);

Event events[MAX_QUEUE_SIZE];
int front = 0, rear = -1, itemCount = 0;
sem_t semaphore;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void enqueue(Event *event) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    if (itemCount == MAX_QUEUE_SIZE) {
        printf("Queue is full\n");
    } else {
        if (rear == MAX_QUEUE_SIZE - 1) rear = -1;
        events[++rear] = *event;
        itemCount++;
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

Event dequeue() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    Event eventToProcess = events[front++];
    if (front == MAX_QUEUE_SIZE) front = 0;
    itemCount--;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    return eventToProcess;
}

void *processEvent(void *arg) {
    EventHandler handlers[] = {NULL}; // replace with your handlers
    while (1) {
        sem_wait(&semaphore);
        Event event = dequeue();
        if (handlers[event.eventType] != NULL) {
            handlers[event.eventType](&event);
        } else {
            printf("No handler found for event type: %d\n", event.eventType);
        }
    }
}

void initializeAndPopulate(int numThreads, EventHandler *handlers) {
    sem_init(&semaphore, 0, 0);
    pthread_t threads[numThreads];
    int i;
    for (i = 0; i < numThreads; i++) {
        if (pthread_create(&threads[i], NULL, &processEvent, NULL) != 0) {
            printf("Failed to create thread\n");
        }
    }
}

void registerEvent(Event *event) {
    enqueue(event);
    sem_post(&semaphore);
}
```
This is a basic implementation where `MAX_QUEUE_SIZE` defines the maximum number of events that can be queued. The array `events` holds these events and `enqueue()`/`dequeue()` functions operate on it as a circular queue. 

The function `processEvent` is run in each thread created by `initializeAndPopulate`. It waits for the semaphore to be posted, which indicates there's work to do, then dequeues an event and calls the appropriate handler. If no handler has been registered for that event type, it prints a warning message instead.

The function `registerEvent` is used to add new events to the queue. It enqueues the event and posts the semaphore, which wakes up one of the waiting threads if any are sleeping (i.e., waiting on the semaphore). 

Please note that this is a simplified version with many limitations and trade-offs. For example, it assumes all handlers take roughly the same time to execute, which might not be true in real systems. Also, error checking has been kept to a minimum for brevity. 

Adding support for dynamic adjustment of thread count based on system load can be done by modifying the `initializeAndPopulate` function, and it involves some form of load-based decision making and potentially using another semaphore or condition variable to signal when a new task is available. It would also involve some kind of thread pooling mechanism.
']

