['Here's an example of how you might structure this in Java. This code assumes you have appropriate error handling and logging mechanisms already defined elsewhere in your program. It doesn't cover all possible scenarios (like different types of objects being loaded instead of `MoSTState`), but it should give you a good starting point:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import org.apache.commons.codec.digest.DigestUtils;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

public class StateLoader {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(StateLoader.class);
    private ConcurrentHashMap<String, Object> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public synchronized Object loadMoSTStateOrHash(String filePath) {
        try (ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream(filePath))) {
            Object obj;
            while ((obj = ois.readObject()) != null) {
                if (obj instanceof MoSTState) {
                    String md5Hash = DigestUtils.md5Hex(filePath);

                    // check if the object is already in the cache and update it, otherwise add it to the cache
                    if (!cache.containsKey(md5Hash)) { 
                        cache.putIfAbsent(md5Hash, obj);

                        logger.info("Successfully loaded MoSTState from file: " + filePath);
                    } else {
                        cache.replace(md5Hash, obj); // updates the existing object in the cache
                        logger.info("Updated cached MoSTState from file: " + filePath);
                    }

                    return obj; 
                }
            }
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            logger.error("Error loading MoSTState from file: " + filePath, e);
        } 

        // if the object is not a MoSTState instance, generate and return its MD5 hash instead
        try {
            MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
            byte[] digest = md.digest(filePath.getBytes());
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();

            for (byte b : digest) {
                sb.append(String.format("%02x", b & 0xff));
            }

            return sb.toString();
        } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
            logger.error("Error generating MD5 hash of string: " + filePath, e);
        }

        return null; // return null if an error occurred during loading or hashing the filePath as a string
    }
}
```
This code uses Java's built-in `ConcurrentHashMap` to cache `MoSTState` objects. The method is synchronized using `this`, which ensures that only one thread can execute this method at a time. This provides basic thread safety for concurrent access. For performance under high load conditions, you may want to look into more sophisticated caching strategies or consider using an external caching solution like Ehcache or Hazelcast IMDG.
']

