import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public int countDisjointGroups(List<Transaction> transactions) {
    long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

    ConcurrentHashMap<String, TransactionGroup> cache = 
        new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    TransactionGroup saleEventTransactionGroup = new TransactionGroup();
    for (Transaction transaction : transactions) {
        if (transaction.isSaleEvent()) {
            String groupId = Arrays.stream(transaction.getProductIds())
                .mapToObj(String::valueOf).sorted()
                .collect(Collectors.joining("_"));

            cache.putIfAbsent(groupId, saleEventTransactionGroup);
        } else {
            // process non-sale event transactions...
        }
    }

    System.out.println("Time taken to process: " + (System.currentTimeMillis() - startTime) + "ms");

    return saleEventTransactionGroup.getGroupCount();
}
```

This code assumes that `productIds` in a Transaction object is an array of unique product IDs, `isSaleEvent` returns true if the transaction was during a sale event, and `String::valueOf` converts product ID to string for sorting. 

Please note that ConcurrentHashMap does not guarantee strong consistency, so if two threads read different versions of data at the same time, both might think they're adding new entries even though one is actually removing old ones. In this specific case this should be fine because we are just counting groups which won't change once counted, but if your scenario differs please make sure to adjust code accordingly. 

Also, it doesn't handle the concurrency issue when updating cache in a proper way and also it is not tested for handling large amount of transactions per second as per requirement. This code needs further tuning and testing before deployment.
