    int largest = INT_MIN;

    for (size_t i = 0; i < length; ++i) {
        if (array[i] > largest) {
            largest = array[i];
        }
    }

    return largest;
}

int main() {
    size_t length;
    int* array = read_integers(&length);

    int largest = find_largest(array, length);

    if (largest < 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Warning: The largest number is negative.\n");
    }

    printf("The largest number in the array is %d\n", largest);

    free(array);
    return 0;
}
```
This program first reads integers from standard input into a dynamically allocated array. The array grows as needed to hold all numbers, and reallocates memory when it gets full. If the array is empty at any point during this process, or if no integers were read in, an error message is printed and the program ends with an error code. The find_largest function then finds and returns the largest number in the array, again checking for edge cases where the array is empty. Finally, the main function prints a warning if the largest number found was negative.
