import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.*;

public class Logger {
    private static final String TOPIC = "logs";
    private static final KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(getProperties());

    public void log(Level level, String message) {
        Future<RecordMetadata> future = producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>(TOPIC, getFormattedMessage(level, message)));
        try {
            future.get();  // This will block until the message is sent (or an exception occurs)
        } catch (InterruptedException | ExecutionException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    private String getFormattedMessage(Level level, String message) {
        return Instant.now() + ": [" + level.toString() + "] - " + message;
    }

    private static Properties getProperties() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "localhost:9092");  // Replace with your Kafka broker address
        props.put("acks", "all");
        props.put("key.serializer", "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
        props.put("value.serializer", "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
        return props;
    }
}
```
In this example, the `log` method formats and sends a log message to Kafka. It uses Kafka's send method which returns a future for the RecordMetadata of the produced record. We block on this future in order to ensure that our application waits until the log has been sent (or an exception occurs).

Please remember to replace "localhost:9092" with your actual Kafka broker address. You should also handle exceptions properly and possibly retry sending messages if necessary. 

The `getFormattedMessage` method formats a log message as a string, including the timestamp, level, and the message itself.

Note that this is just a very basic example and does not include many of the features you mentioned. You would need to implement these features yourself or find an existing library that meets your needs.
