["Here is an example of how you could implement this in JavaScript using ES6 syntax and promises for thread safety and external logging service integration:

```javascript
class DataCopier {
  constructor(maxBufferSize, logger) {
    this.totalBytesCopied = 0;
    this.maxBufferSize = maxBufferSize;
    this.logger = logger;
  }

  copyData(dataChunks, numBytesToCopy) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      if (numBytesToCopy > this.maxBufferSize) {
        reject('Requested data size exceeds maximum buffer size');
      } else {
        let copiedBytes = 0;
        const newBuffer = Buffer.alloc(numBytesToCopy);

        for (let chunk of dataChunks) {
          if (copiedBytes + chunk.length <= numBytesToCopy) {
            chunk.copy(newBuffer, copiedBytes, 0, chunk.length);
            copiedBytes += chunk.length;
          } else {
            const remaining = numBytesToCopy - copiedBytes;
            chunk.copy(newBuffer, copiedBytes, 0, remaining);
            copiedBytes = numBytesToCopy;
            break;
          }
        }

        this.totalBytesCopied += numBytesToCopy;
        if (this.logger) {
          this.logger.log(`${numBytesToCopy} bytes have been copied. Total bytes copied so far: ${this.totalBytesCopied}`);
        }

        resolve(newBuffer);
      }
    });
  }
}
```

This class has a constructor that takes the maximum buffer size and an optional logger as parameters. It uses a promise-based approach to ensure thread safety, because Promises in JavaScript are designed to handle concurrent operations without causing race conditions or data corruption. The `copyData` method accepts a list of buffered data chunks and a number of bytes to copy from these chunks, then returns a new buffer with the copied data. 

Note that this is a simple implementation for illustrative purposes. In real-world scenarios, you would need to consider additional factors such as error handling, input validation, memory management, etc.
