['Here's a basic implementation in JavaScript using async/await for handling asynchronous data streams and promises to manage completion or failure of asynchronous operations. Note that this function does not actually copy the bytes from chunks into a new buffer but rather demonstrates the concept and interaction of multiple parts of the system. The actual implementation would depend on the specifics of your logging system, error handling strategy, and so forth.

```javascript
class DataCopier {
  constructor(logger) {
    this.logger = logger;
  }

  async copyData(chunks, numBytes) {
    // Track time to complete operation
    const startTime = Date.now();

    let bytesRead = 0;
    let bufferIndex = 0;
    const newBuffer = Buffer.allocUnsafe(numBytes);

    for (let chunk of chunks) {
      if (bytesRead >= numBytes) break; // Enough data read, exit loop

      await this.logger.info(`Reading from chunk: ${chunk}`);

      const bytesRemainingInChunk = chunk.length - chunk.readOffset;
      const bytesToCopy = Math.min(bytesRemainingInChunk, numBytes - bytesRead);

      chunk.copy(newBuffer, bufferIndex, 0, bytesToCopy);
      chunk.readOffset += bytesToCopy; // Update the read offset for next iteration

      await this.logger.info(`Copied ${bytesToCopy} bytes from chunk`);
      bufferIndex += bytesToCopy; // Move to next position in newBuffer
      bytesRead += bytesToCopy; // Keep track of total bytes copied
    }

    if (bytesRead < numBytes) {
      await this.logger.error(`Not enough data, only ${bytesRead} bytes read`);
      throw new Error("Failed to copy enough data");
    }

    // Log completion and time taken
    const endTime = Date.now();
    await this.logger.info(`Finished copying ${numBytes} bytes in ${endTime - startTime}ms`);

    return newBuffer;
  }
}
```
In the above code, we assume each chunk is an object with a `readOffset` property that tracks how much has been read from it. Each time a chunk is read, its offset is updated and when enough bytes are read to fulfill the request, reading stops and a new buffer containing copied data is returned. The function logs information regarding each step of the process through the logger interface.

Please note this code snippet doesn't handle all edge cases like checking for available memory before copying large amount of data or handling concurrent access as it needs to be more specific about how your data chunks, buffers are managed and logging is done. Also error handling can be improved with appropriate try-catch blocks and proper error types. This code also assumes that the `copy` method on chunk objects actually performs the copy operation correctly and efficiently.
