["Here's an implementation in JavaScript utilizing ArrayBuffers for handling binary data efficiently:

```javascript
class BufferManager {
  constructor() {
    this.dataChunks = [];
    this.currentOffset = 0;
    this.cumulativeSize = 0;
  }

  append(arrayBuffer) {
    if (!(arrayBuffer instanceof ArrayBuffer)) {
      throw new Error('Only array buffers can be added');
    }
    this.dataChunks.push(new Uint8Array(arrayBuffer));
    this.cumulativeSize += arrayBuffer.byteLength;
  }

  readBytes(count) {
    if (this.currentOffset + count > this.cumulativeSize) {
      throw new Error('Requested byte count exceeds available data');
    }

    const result = [];
    let remainingCount = count;
    for (let i = 0; i < this.dataChunks.length && remainingCount > 0; i++) {
      if (this.currentOffset >= this.dataChunks[i].byteLength) {
        // Skip the current chunk if we've already read it entirely.
        continue;
      }

      const start = Math.max(this.currentOffset, 0);
      const end = Math.min(start + remainingCount, this.dataChunks[i].byteLength);
      result.push(...this.dataChunks[i].slice(start, end));

      this.currentOffset += (end - start);
      remainingCount -= (end - start);
    }

    return new Uint8Array(result);
  }
}
```
In this code, a `BufferManager` class is defined which keeps track of all data chunks and the current offset within them. Data can be appended to the manager using the `append()` method, and read using the `readBytes()` method. The function ensures that no more bytes are requested than available, maintaining integrity of the data.
"]

