["Here is a basic implementation of such a function in JavaScript using async/await and Buffer. Please note that this solution assumes that your data chunks are ArrayBuffers or TypedArrays instances:

```js
async function copyBytesAsync(chunkArray, byteCount) {
  if (byteCount === 0) return new Uint8Array(); // Return an empty array for zero-byte requests

  let bytesCopied = 0;
  const outputBuffer = new Uint8Array(Math.min(byteCount, chunkArray.length));

  for (const chunk of chunkArray) {
    if (bytesCopied >= byteCount) break; // Stop copying if we've reached the requested byte count

    // Get the maximum possible bytes to copy from this chunk without exceeding the request
    const chunkBytes = chunk.byteLength - (chunkArray[0] === chunk ? 0 : 1);
    const bytesToCopy = Math.min(chunkBytes, byteCount - bytesCopied);

    if (bytesToCopy <= 0) continue; // Skip this chunk if it has no bytes to copy

    outputBuffer.set(new Uint8Array(chunk, 0, bytesToCopy), bytesCopied);
    bytesCopied += bytesToCopy;

    await new Promise(resolve => setImmediate(resolve)); // Yield control for other tasks in the event loop
  }

  return outputBuffer.slice(0, byteCount); // Return only the maximum possible bytes available
}
```
This function creates a single Uint8Array as the target buffer and fills it with data from the input chunks one by one. It uses set() method to efficiently copy chunk's data into outputBuffer. Asynchronous operations are performed using Promises and setImmediate, which is useful for handling high load conditions and improving responsiveness.
"]

