["Here's a basic implementation in C. Note that real-world implementations would be significantly more comprehensive due to the many factors involved (e.g., support for different types of connections, handling of SSL, graceful shutdowns, etc.) and will likely require libraries/frameworks such as libevent or Boost::Asio:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct {
    char client_address[INET6_ADDRSTRLEN]; // for IPv4/IPv6
    int server_port;
    enum {
        CONNECTED, 
        DISCONNECTED
    } connection_state;
    time_t last_active_time;
} tcp_connection_struct;

typedef struct {
    tcp_connection_struct** pool;
    size_t capacity;
    size_t size;
    pthread_mutex_t lock;
} tcp_pool;

tcp_pool* init_pool(size_t initial_capacity) {
    tcp_pool* new_pool = malloc(sizeof(tcp_pool));
    new_pool->capacity = initial_capacity;
    new_pool->size = 0;
    new_pool->pool = calloc(initial_capacity, sizeof(tcp_connection_struct*));

    pthread_mutex_init(&new_pool->lock, NULL);

    return new_pool;
}

void add_to_pool(tcp_pool* pool, tcp_connection_struct* connection) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&pool->lock);

    if (pool->size == pool->capacity) {
        pool->capacity *= 2;
        pool->pool = realloc(pool->pool, sizeof(tcp_connection_struct*)*pool->capacity);
    }

    pool->pool[pool->size++] = connection;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&pool->lock);
}

void remove_from_pool(tcp_pool* pool, int index) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&pool->lock);

    free(pool->pool[index]);
    memmove(&pool->pool[index], &pool->pool[index + 1], (pool->size - index - 1)*sizeof(tcp_connection_struct*));
    pool->size--;

    if ((pool->capacity > 2*pool->size) && (pool->capacity > 8)) {
        pool->capacity /= 2;
        pool->pool = realloc(pool->pool, sizeof(tcp_connection_struct*)*pool->capacity);
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&pool->lock);
}
``` 
This code initializes a connection pool with the specified initial capacity. It provides functions for adding and removing connections from the pool, ensuring thread safety by locking on the mutex in each function call. Dynamic resizing of the pool is handled such that it doubles the size when full and halves it when it's 1/4th-full, but only if capacity remains at least 8 (to prevent frequent reallocations for small pools). The actual handling of connections and their timeouts would be more complicated, involving separate threads or event loops.

This is a very simplified example and not meant to be a complete solution. Real world implementations would require further abstraction and additional error checking. Also, it doesn't handle logging - that would typically be handled by an external library such as glog or log4cxx.
"]

