["This is quite complex task to solve within a single response due to many factors such as dynamic scaling, efficient memory management, error handling and thread safety among others. However, I will present a simplified version of the function and some pseudo-code for managing TCP connections in C. This doesn't cover all your requirements but it should give you a good starting point.

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <sys/time.h>

typedef struct connection {
    int client_address;
    int server_port;
    char* connection_state;
    struct timeval last_active_time;
} Connection;

Connection* connections = NULL; // array of connections 
int numConnections = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

// Connect to a remote server and create a new connection structure.
Connection* establishConnection(int client_address, int server_port) {
    Connection* conn = (Connection*)malloc(sizeof(Connection));

    // Establish the connection here...

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    connections = realloc(connections, ++numConnections * sizeof(Connection*));
    connections[numConnections - 1] = conn;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

    return conn;
}

// Update the last active time of a connection.
void updateLastActiveTime(Connection* conn) {
    gettimeofday(&conn->last_active_time, NULL);
}

// Close and free a connection.
void closeConnection(Connection* conn) {
    // Close the connection here...

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    for (int i = 0; i < numConnections; ++i) {
        if (connections[i] == conn) {
            connections[i] = connections[--numConnections];
            free(conn);
            break;
        }
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This code maintains an array of `Connection` structures and manages them in a thread-safe manner. When establishing a connection, the function allocates memory for a new connection structure and adds it to the array. When closing a connection, the function searches the array for the corresponding connection and frees its memory before removing it from the array.

This is just a basic example and doesn't include error handling or timeouts. For these features, you would need to use system-level functions such as `select` or `poll` in combination with a mechanism like `gettimeofday` for tracking idle connections or `sigaction` for handling signals (like SIGALRM) and managing timeouts.
