// Struct to hold event data and config
typedef struct {
    int LOG_LEVEL;
    size_t BUFFER_SIZE;
    char *BACKUP_DESTINATION;
} LogConfig;

typedef struct {
    char *eventType;
    void *payload;
    int priority;
    time_t timestamp;
} EventData;

// Global variables for log file and mutex to handle concurrency 
FILE *logFile = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t logMutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void flexible_event_processor(LogConfig config, EventData event) {
    // Format the metadata header
    char header[256]; 
    snprintf(header, sizeof(header), "%ld [%s] %d: ", 
             event.timestamp, event.eventType, event.priority);

    // Lock mutex before writing to log file
    pthread_mutex_lock(&logMutex);

    if (logFile == NULL) {
        logFile = fopen(config.BACKUP_DESTINATION, "a");
        if (logFile == NULL) {
            printf("Failed to open log file\n");
            return;
        }
    }

    // Write header and payload to the log file
    fwrite(header, sizeof(char), strlen(header), logFile);
    fwrite(event.payload, sizeof(char), config.BUFFER_SIZE - strlen(header), logFile);

    // Flush the buffer to ensure data is written immediately
    fflush(logFile);

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&logMutex); 
}
```
This code provides a basic implementation of your requirements. It starts by defining necessary structs for event data and log configuration. Then it defines a global variable `logFile` to hold the file pointer for the log file, which is opened when the first event is processed and kept open throughout the application's lifecycle. The mutex `logMutex` is used to ensure thread safety while writing to the log file. 

Inside the `flexible_event_processor` function, it formats a metadata header using `snprintf`, locks the mutex, writes the formatted header and payload to the log file, and finally flushes the buffer to ensure data is written immediately. 

