['Writing a complete system with all these features is a large task that can't be fully implemented here due to complexity and size considerations. However, I can provide a simple example of the `readSortAndProcessFile` function which demonstrates some of these features using pthreads for thread safety and OpenMP for parallelization:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <omp.h>

#define MAX_NUM 100

typedef struct {
    int* data;
    size_t size;
} Result;

// Lock for thread safety
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

Result readSortAndProcessFile(const char* filename) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    FILE* file = fopen(filename, "r");
    if (file == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to open file\n");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    int num;
    size_t count = 0;
    while (fscanf(file, "%d", &num) == 1) {
        if (num < 0 || num > MAX_NUM) {
            printf("Value out of range: %d\n", num);
        } else {
            count++;
        }
    }

    int* data = malloc(count * sizeof(*data));
    if (data == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to allocate memory\n");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    rewind(file);
    count = 0;
    while (fscanf(file, "%d", &num) == 1) {
        if (num >= 0 && num <= MAX_NUM) {
            data[count++] = num;
        }
    }

    fclose(file);

    // Sort the array in parallel using OpenMP
    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)
    for (size_t i = 0; i < count; i++) {
        int min = data[i];
        size_t idx = i;

        for (size_t j = i + 1; j < count; j++) {
            if (data[j] < min) {
                min = data[j];
                idx = j;
            }
        }

        int temp = data[i];
        data[i] = data[idx];
        data[idx] = temp;
    }

    Result result = {data, count};
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

    return result;
}
``` 

This code only reads a file containing integers separated by spaces or newline characters and puts them in an array. It checks if the values are within a predefined range (0-100). The function is thread safe using pthread's mutex locking mechanism, preventing race conditions during concurrent access by multiple threads.

The sorting operation is done with OpenMP for parallelization. This can drastically speed up the process on multi-core machines as each core will be responsible for a different part of the array. 

Note: In practice, this code lacks many features requested in the task description such as logging, error handling, dynamic memory allocation strategies, integration with external libraries and so on. For more complex requirements, we would likely need to use more robust and extensive tools or libraries.
']

